Dirty checking is Hibernate's automatic change detection. When an entity is loaded, Hibernate takes a SNAPSHOT (deep copy of all field values). At flush time (before commit), Hibernate compares EVERY managed entity's current state against its snapshot. If any field changed, it generates an UPDATE SQL. This happens for EVERY managed entity in the persistence context.

```java
@Transactional
public void updateBalance(String accountId, BigDecimal newBalance) {
    Account account = accountRepo.findById(accountId).orElseThrow();
    // Hibernate takes snapshot: {id=SAV2001, balance=125000, version=1}

    account.setBalance(newBalance);
    // NO explicit save() needed! Dirty checking detects the change.

    // At commit: Hibernate compares current state vs snapshot:
    // current: {balance=100000} vs snapshot: {balance=125000} → DIRTY!
    // Generates: UPDATE accounts SET balance=100000, version=2 WHERE id='SAV2001' AND version=1
}
```

The cost of dirty checking: for N loaded entities with M fields each, Hibernate performs N × M comparisons at every flush. Loading 1000 Account entities with 20 fields each = 20,000 field comparisons. Plus, each entity's snapshot consumes memory (a complete copy of all field values).

```java
// ═══ readOnly=true — skip dirty checking ═══
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public List<TradeDTO> getSettledTrades(String accountId) {
    List<Trade> trades = tradeRepo.findByAccountIdAndStatus(accountId, "SETTLED");
    // Hibernate loads trades BUT does NOT take snapshots (readOnly=true).
    // At commit: NO dirty checking runs (nothing to compare — no snapshots).
    // Savings: no snapshot memory, no comparison CPU.

    return trades.stream().map(this::toDTO).toList();
    // Map to DTO inside the transaction — BEFORE session closes.
}

// ═══ readOnly=true also optimizes at DB level ═══
// PostgreSQL: read-only transaction uses MVCC snapshot with lighter locking.
// MySQL: InnoDB skips undo log for read-only transactions.
// Connection pool: read-only hint can route to read replica in master-slave setup.
```

Open Session in View (OSIV) is a Spring Boot filter (`OpenEntityManagerInViewInterceptor`) that keeps the JPA EntityManager (Hibernate Session) open for the ENTIRE HTTP request lifecycle — from controller entry through JSON serialization in the response.

```java
// WITH OSIV (default: spring.jpa.open-in-view=true):
@GetMapping("/accounts/{id}")
public Account getAccount(@PathVariable String id) {
    Account account = accountService.findById(id);  // @Transactional ends here
    // Session still OPEN (OSIV!) — JSON serialization triggers lazy loads:
    // account.getTrades() → fires SQL (N+1 hidden in view layer!)
    return account;  // lazy loads work — no LazyInitializationException
    // BUT: DB connection held during entire JSON serialization!
}

// WITHOUT OSIV (spring.jpa.open-in-view=false):
@GetMapping("/accounts/{id}")
public Account getAccount(@PathVariable String id) {
    Account account = accountService.findById(id);  // @Transactional + session CLOSED
    return account;  // account.getTrades() → LazyInitializationException!
    // FIX: return a DTO, or use JOIN FETCH in the service method.
}
```

Three problems with OSIV in production. First, DB connections are held for the entire request duration including JSON serialization (which can be slow for large responses). Under load, the connection pool is exhausted — requests queue waiting for connections. Second, lazy loads during serialization fire N+1 queries that are invisible to the service layer — the developer doesn't see them in the service code, only in SQL logs. Third, accidental writes during view rendering could persist without an explicit @Transactional boundary.

```yaml
# DISABLE OSIV — recommended for production:
spring:
  jpa:
    open-in-view: false
# Spring Boot logs a warning at startup if OSIV is enabled:
# "spring.jpa.open-in-view is enabled by default. Therefore, database queries
#  may be performed during view rendering."
```

